---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ReferenceBlock from '@components/ReferenceBlock.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { toolPath, categoryPath } from '@lib/paths';
import { toolPathBySlug } from '@lib/tools/tool-link';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find(c => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = toolPath({ slug: config.slug, segment: category.segment });
const categoryHref = categoryPath(category);
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>What is chess tac toe? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Chess tac toe is a 4 by 4 board for two people.
      Each player has a pawn, a rook, a knight, and a bishop.
    </p>
    <p>
      You play chess tac toe on this page by passing one phone.
      Chess tic tac toe and hybrid chess tic tac toe name the same hybrid board game.
    </p>
    <p>
      Chess piece tic tac toe still ends on four in a line.
      It does not end on checkmate.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Open Chess Tac Toe</a>
  </section>

  <section id="why-one-device">
    <h2>Why does one phone matter? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      One phone matters because both players are already in the room.
      Passing one phone across a table when nobody has a chess set is the ordinary case.
    </p>
    <p>
      Playing a full match without creating an account keeps the score in the open page, and nothing is saved.
      A small board is enough for four pieces each.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href={toolPathBySlug('tic-tac-toe')}>Tic tac toe</a> is the same hot seat with plain marks.
      <a href={toolPathBySlug('connect-4')}>Connect 4</a> is the gravity board when you want discs instead of pieces.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="how-a-turn-works">
    <h2>How does a turn work? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      The banner says place while you still hold a piece, and move after the hand is empty.
      Pick a letter, then tap an empty square.
    </p>
    <p>
      For example, X places a pawn on the top left and O places a pawn on a different square.
      X then places the rook, the knight, and the bishop.
      After that fourth placement, X moves even if O still has a piece to place.
    </p>
    <p>
      A legal move follows the piece.
      An illegal tap does nothing, and the note names the rule.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="rules">
    <h2>Which moves are legal? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      A legal move is the one the piece can actually make.
      Piece movement is checked on every slide, and placement is only onto an empty square.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>A knight moves in an L and can jump</li>
      <li>A rook moves in a straight line and cannot pass through a piece</li>
      <li>A bishop moves on a diagonal and cannot pass through a piece</li>
      <li>A pawn steps one square toward the other player, or captures one square diagonally forward</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      For example, a knight on the corner can reach the square two across and one down.
      Any other step from that corner is refused.
    </p>
    <p>
      For example, a pawn that is already on its far rank turns around on the next move and keeps the new direction until it sits on the new far rank.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="checkmate">
    <h2>Why is this not checkmate? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Checkmate does not win, and the page does not look for it.
      Four of your own pieces in a line is the only win.
    </p>
    <p>
      Unlike chess, capturing a piece sends it back to that player hand.
      Their next turn is a placement again.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="key-idea" heading="One ruleset">
      <p>The win is four in a line. There is no second mode for check.</p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <p>
      Expecting checkmate to end the match is the wrong reading.
      This board lives in the <a href={categoryHref}>games category</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="illegal">
    <h2>What does the note say about an illegal tap? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      The note names the rule, and the board stays as it was.
      The common mistake is placing on a square that is already taken. You cannot capture by placing.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Placing on an occupied square">
      <p>
        Placing a piece on a square the other player already occupies does not take that piece.
        Pick an empty square instead.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Sliding through a piece">
      <p>
        Moving a rook through another piece is refused.
        The same block stops a bishop. A knight is the piece that jumps.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <p>
      Waiting to move until the other player has placed all four gives away turns you already have.
      Once your hand is empty, you move.
    </p>
    <p>
      Undo takes back one placement or one move.
      However, the score stays when you rematch, and the other player moves first.
    </p>
    <p>
      Taking back a placement that landed on the wrong square returns that piece to your hand.
      Learning which square a knight can reach from a corner is the move the note is there to protect.
    </p>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>

<style>
  .cta-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    height: var(--touch-target);
    padding-inline: var(--space-5);
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
    background: var(--color-accent);
    color: var(--color-text-inverse);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: var(--transition-color);
  }
  .cta-link:hover { opacity: 0.9; text-decoration: none; }
</style>
